    "q-278351": {
      "dom_id": "step-q278351",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "From **left to right,** the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{4}=1$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand \n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(0+4)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}-4 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5} & =5 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =25 \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm 5 \\\\ x & =-4\\pm 5 \\\\ x & =-9,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $(-9,0)$ and $(1,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
