    "q-278364": {
      "dom_id": "step-q278364",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "From **top to bottom,** the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}=1$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (y-5)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-5 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =5\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,8)$ and $(0,2).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
