    "q-278387": {
      "dom_id": "step-q278387",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "The hyperbola ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1$ intersects the line $x=1$ at\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $).$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $x=1,$ we substitute $x=1$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (1)^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ -{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the hyperbola intersects the vertical line $x=1$  at $(1,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
